Yes. Actually, if I were a junior Wix developer in 2026, I would change the reading list quite significantly.

I wouldn't throw away the original list, because books about JavaScript, design principles, UX and clean code are still valuable.

But I'd shift my priorities.

As a Wix developer, I wouldn't just want to know how to build websites. I'd want to become someone who can design, develop, customize, optimize and maintain professional websites for clients and businesses.

I'd aim to become more than someone who knows how to use the Wix editor.

My goal would be to become a well-rounded Wix Studio developer with strong UI/UX, frontend development, SEO and problem-solving skills.

## 1. The books I'd prioritize as a Wix developer

I'd organize them into five areas.

### A. UI/UX and visual design — extremely important

Bookshelf

1. Refactoring UI

My number-one design recommendation.

Learn spacing, typography, color systems, visual hierarchy and professional-looking layouts.

Why: Wix makes it relatively easy to assemble a website. Knowing how to make that website look professionally designed is what separates an average Wix builder from a good web designer.

10 лучших книг о дизайне для дизайнера

2. Don't Make Me Think

Learn usability, navigation, information architecture and user behavior.

Why: Your clients don't just need attractive websites. They need websites that convert visitors into customers.

Non-Designer's Design Book by Robin P. Williams | Goodreads

3. The Non-Designer's Design Book

Learn contrast, repetition, alignment and proximity.

Why: These principles are useful when designing everything from a simple business website to a sophisticated landing page.

### B. HTML, CSS and JavaScript — your technical foundation

Learning Web Design: A Beginner's Guide To Html, Css, Javascript, And Web Images Book By Jennifer Robbins,aaron Gustafson, ('tp') | Indigo

4. Learning Web Design

Understand HTML, CSS, JavaScript and responsive web design.

Why: Even if Wix handles much of the underlying infrastructure, understanding what happens behind the editor helps you troubleshoot and customize websites.

CSS in Depth, Second Edition - Keith J. Grant

5. CSS in Depth

Learn CSS layouts, positioning, responsive techniques and modern styling.

Why: This is especially valuable when using Wix Studio's custom CSS capabilities.

Eloquent JavaScript, 4th Edition by Marijn Haverbeke - Penguin Books Australia

6. Eloquent JavaScript

Learn JavaScript fundamentals, functions, objects, arrays, promises and asynchronous programming.

Why: This is essential when you start using Velo, custom interactions, CMS queries and API integrations.

### C. Wix-specific development and problem-solving

Here I'd actually prioritize official documentation over buying lots of Wix-specific books.

The platform changes frequently, and documentation is more likely to reflect the current APIs and workflows.

I'd concentrate on:

- Wix Studio Editor and responsive layouts.
- Wix CMS and dynamic pages.
- Velo development.
- Frontend and backend JavaScript.
- Wix APIs and third-party integrations.
- GitHub and version control.

One particularly important distinction for 2026: Wix is transitioning from its older Velo APIs toward the newer Wix JavaScript SDK. Existing Velo skills remain useful, but I'd learn the SDK for new development rather than treating Velo as the entire future of Wix development.

